<section>

<h1>Research</h1>

<p>
I am interested in how humans search, learn, and make decisions under uncertainty.
My research combines behavioural experiments, computational modelling,
and cognitive neuroscience to understand adaptive behaviour in complex environments.
</p>

<h3>Research Interests</h3>
<ul>
<li>Decision Making</li>
<li>Computational Neuroscience</li>
<li>Foraging Behaviour</li>
<li>Information Foraging</li>
</ul>
<hr>

<h2>Environmental Structure and Foraging</h2>

<p>
How does the structure of an environment influence decision making?
</p>

<p>
My master's thesis investigated whether structured and random environments lead
to different patterns of patch-leaving behaviour in a reward-guided foraging task.
The work combines behavioural experiments, anxiety measures, and computational modelling.
</p>

<ul>
<li>2D Patch Foraging Task</li>
<li>Hierarchical Drift Diffusion Models</li>
<li>Linear Mixed Models</li>
<li>Behavioural Analysis</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>Smartphone Information Foraging</h2>

<p>
Humans increasingly forage for information through smartphones rather than physical environments.
</p>

<p>
This project explores how affective states, environmental cues,
and uncertainty influence digital information search behaviour.
</p>

<ul>
<li>Digital Information Foraging</li>
<li>Experimental Design</li>
<li>Interface Prototyping</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h2>Computational Modelling of Behaviour</h2>

<ul>
<li>Hierarchical Drift Diffusion Models (HDDM)</li>
<li>Generalized Linear Mixed Models (GLMM)</li>
<li>Bayesian Regression Models</li>
<li>Reinforcement Learning Models</li>
</ul>

</section>
